    ('climate-change', 3, 'Sacco has a recorded pro-climate-compliance vote: in September 2022, when the council voted 4-3 to join a 13-city lawsuit seeking to halt Oregon''s Climate Friendly and Equitable Communities rules (parking reform, EV-charging mandates, tree-canopy requirements), Sacco was one of the three dissenters voting against suing the state — the dissenting bloc argued the money was better spent working toward the climate goals than on litigation. She also participated in the council''s unanimous early-2022 vote awarding the $280,000 Climate Action Plan contract that produced Tualatin''s Community Climate Action Plan, adopted by the council in May 2024 with a roadmap to citywide net-zero emissions by 2050 through energy efficiency, renewable energy, and more sustainable transportation. Supporting compliance with state climate rules and a 2050 net-zero municipal roadmap matches the invest-in-clean-energy-while-gradually-reducing-fossil-fuel-reliance chair; she has not advocated a 2030 phase-out or emergency declaration, nor a market-only approach.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/tualatin-among-13-cities-suing-oregon-to-halt-new-climate-regulations/','https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/city-of-tualatin-awards-climate-action-plan-contract/','https://tualatinoregon.gov/administration/climate-action/']::text[]),
    ('civil-rights', 2, 'Equity and inclusion are Sacco''s signature issue, backed by institutional action rather than statements alone. She cites creating the Inclusion, Diversity, Equity, and Access (I.D.E.A.) Advisory Committee — chartered in 2023 to advocate for underserved populations, identify opportunities, and recommend measurable goals — as a first-term accomplishment, and her Council Corner columns (Aug 2023, Dec 2024) center the Tualatin 2030 Vision to "cultivate a caring and inclusive community that promotes equity, diversity, belonging and access," celebrating Pride and ¡Viva Tualatin! events. In February 2025 she voted with the 6-1 majority — over a colleague''s dissent — to proclaim March 31 Transgender Day of Visibility and spoke in support of the motion. Her 2020 platform pressed language access (translation of city materials, interpretation at meetings), arguing "City leaders cannot serve the people of Tualatin until they have the ability to serve all the people of Tualatin." Building standing equity institutions and measuring progress goes beyond maintaining current law, placing her at the actively-address-systemic-inequity chair, though she has proposed no mandates or reparations.', ARRAY['https://hillsboronewstimes.com/2024/09/13/sacco-seeks-second-term-on-tualatin-city-council/','https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/council-corner-august-2023/','https://tualatinlife.com/featured/tualatin-to-recognize-transgender-day-of-visibility/']::text[]),
